WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.899
Fritz Haber made food from the air.

00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:08.450
He invented a process that takes nitrogen
from the air and turns it into fertilizer.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:14.590
This revolutionized agriculture and fed billions
of people who otherwise might have starved.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:18.760
He became a hero in his native Germany and
won a Nobel prize.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.960
Yet the man who fed billions also had a hand
in unspeakable atrocities.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:29.620
Fritz Haber developed poison gas which was
then used on both sides in World War One.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:34.010
His work led to the development of the devastating
use of gas during the Holocaust.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:40.400
Haber himself was born to a Jewish family
in 1868 in Breslau, a city that was then part

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.010
of Germany but is now located in Poland and
is known as Wroclaw.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.469
He grew up without his mother, who died weeks
after giving birth.

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:51.559
Fritz’s father, Siegfried was devastated.

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:55.800
He threw himself into his business and became
a prosperous dye merchant.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.680
Siegfried eventually remarried and had three
daughters.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:03.680
He doted on his girls but not on Fritz…who
was a reminder of the pain of losing his first

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:04.680
wife.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.850
After Fritz graduated from an elite school
and celebrated with friends in a pub until

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:14.540
the wee hours of the morning, his dad was
not happy when he overslept and skipped family

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:15.540
breakfast.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:20.430
Fritz’s father took his three girls to Fritz’s
bedroom and said: “Look well!

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.330
This is how the life of a drunkard begins!”.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.640
That deeply hurt his son.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.030
Fritz wanted out of his father’s house.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.470
And out of Breslau.

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:35.880
He wanted to “break free of the chains that
wear me down…” as he wrote to a friend.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.790
He decided to go to university to study chemistry;
he had already been conducting homemade experiments

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.250
at his aunt’s house.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.399
He studied at several German universities
and earned a doctorate in organic chemistry

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:47.560
in 1891.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:52.390
Haber also served a year in the military and
hoped to be commissioned as an officer in

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:57.659
the reserves, but he likely understood that
he was rejected because of his Jewish heritage.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:02.579
He later converted to Christianity, possibly
to advance his career or to assimilate into

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:06.290
German society, where anti-Semitism was rising.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.500
Rejection became part of his life.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:13.390
When he applied to join the lab of the esteemed
physical chemist Wilhelm Ostwald in Leipzig,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.780
his application was denied, while that of
his friend and fellow student, Richard Abegg,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.200
was accepted.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.720
No one appeared to recognize his brilliance.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:27.310
He moved from job to job, working at factories
in Eastern Europe through his dad’s connections.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:33.350
Finally, in 1894, at the age of 26, he was
hired as an assistant at the Technical University

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:38.550
of Karlsruhe, now the Karlsruhe Institute
of Technology, in the department of Chemical

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.500
and Fuel Technology.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.460
The university wasn't renowned at the time,
but this worked in his favor, as his supervisors

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.640
gave him the freedom to pursue his own ideas.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.540
When someone asked how he managed to master
a range of specialties in chemistry so quickly,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:58.760
Haber replied that he “studied every night
until 2 am until I got it.”

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.530
Sometimes, he’d escape into sanatoriums
to calm his nerves.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:08.959
He worked so hard that his wife, chemist Clara
Immerwahr wrote: “Fritz is so scattered,

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:13.200
if I didn’t bring him to his son every once
in a while, he wouldn’t even know that he

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.680
was a father.” (page 63)

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.629
Clara was also the child of a prosperous merchant.

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:22.180
She was also born to a Jewish family and later
converted to Christianity.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.830
Because she couldn’t obtain good schooling
at a young age due to her gender, she made

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.040
up for it with private tutors.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.129
At first, she was only allowed to attend university
as a guest, as women were not legally admissible

00:03:33.129 --> 00:03:34.439
at the time.

00:03:34.439 --> 00:03:40.100
When that policy changed, she became the first
woman in Germany to earn a Ph.D. in chemistry.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.890
Yet her career was overshadowed by her husband’s;
she couldn’t advance in academia as she

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.879
was expected to fulfill the role of the professor’s
wife.

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:52.930
Fritz Haber was working at the university
when a British scientist issued a dire warning

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:54.950
in 1898.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:59.459
Sir William Crookes warned that “England
and all civilized nations stand in deadly

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:02.610
peril of not having enough to eat.”

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:07.860
He focused his concern on the “bread-eaters
of the whole world”, the “great Caucasian

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.860
race”.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:13.930
He predicted that the world would face famine
within decades unless a new source of nitrogen

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:17.640
for agricultural fertilizers could be found.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.590
Nitrogen is a vital nutrient for plant growth.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:25.759
Scientists relied on limited natural sources
of nitrogen to fertilize soil, such as animal

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:31.340
manure, bird and bat droppings, or sodium
nitrate from Chilean mines - but these resources

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.199
are limited.

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:38.390
Nitrogen is abundant in the atmosphere; 78%
of the air is made up of nitrogen.

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:43.570
However, in the way that humans can’t drink
saltwater, plants can’t use nitrogen directly

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:48.460
from the air as a source of nutrition, because
of its strong triple bond that makes it difficult

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.770
for plants to digest.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.139
It must be converted into a more useful form.

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:57.930
Haber discovered that hydrogen breaks the
strong triple bond of nitrogen and converts

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:03.860
it to ammonia, or NH3, which is easier for
plants to absorb and use.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:09.180
Ammonia is the raw material for common nitrogen-based
fertilizers, providing plants with essential

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.110
nutrients for growth, increasing crop yields
and food production.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:18.410
Haber actually struggled at first to find
the point at which hydrogen and nitrogen atoms

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:20.430
combined into ammonia.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:25.110
He almost gave up until a rival scientist
insulted him.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:30.120
Walter Nernst said: “It is unfortunate that
so little ammonia is formed in this state

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:36.509
of equilibrium, compared with what Haber’s
highly inaccurate numbers led us to assume.”

00:05:36.509 --> 00:05:41.580
That really ticked Haber off; he became obsessed
with obtaining ammonia before Nernst did.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.720
Haber tried using extreme heat to encourage
the formation of ammonia, but that didn’t

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:47.720
work.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:52.419
Even 1000 degrees Celsius only produced a
minuscule amount of ammonia: one-hundredth

00:05:52.419 --> 00:05:54.300
of one percent.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:06:04.090
Haber found an ally in Germany’s largest
chemical company BASF which gave him a generous

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:07.580
salary to pay for lab equipment and hire assistants.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.380
In March 1909, he achieved a breakthrough

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:16.720
Haber used a catalyst, osmium, to accelerate
the chemical reaction between nitrogen and

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:17.720
hydrogen.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.550
They combined to produce ammonia with a yield
of around 6%.

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:26.370
Haber had discovered a process akin to turning
lead into gold.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:33.190
It would make him fabulously rich; BASF agreed
to pay him 1.5 pfennig - the German cent - in

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:38.630
royalties for each kilo of ammonia produced
- which added up to a significant sum as the

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.949
large-scale production of ammonia followed.

00:06:41.949 --> 00:06:47.960
A chemist and engineer at BASF, Carl Bosch,
was responsible for scaling up and commercializing

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:49.840
the production of ammonia.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.900
The process of synthesizing ammonia is known
worldwide as the Haber-Bosch process.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:03.150
Today, an estimated 150 million metric tons
of nitrogen is extracted from the air annually,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:08.210
transformed into ammonia, and distributed
as nourishing fertilizer for crops.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.810
There are long-term environmental concerns
to consider.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:15.960
Chemicals from these fertilizers can find
their way into water systems, harming water

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:19.220
quality and the surrounding ecosystems.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:23.919
But without the Haber-Bosch process, it’s
likely that billions of people would not have

00:07:23.919 --> 00:07:28.120
been able to obtain the food and nutrition
they need to survive.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:33.550
About half of our planet’s seven billion
people are fed with crops grown using synthetic

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:34.810
fertilizers.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:39.930
In 1911, two years after his groundbreaking
discovery, Haber became the founding director

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:44.720
of a prestigious new research institution,
the Kaiser Wilhelm Institute for Physical

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:46.620
Chemistry and Electrochemistry.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:52.340
This photo shows Haber at the opening ceremony,
steps behind the German emperor and King of

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.050
Prussia, who lent his name to the institute.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.362
Haber was now a member of the nation’s elite.

00:07:58.362 --> 00:08:02.030
And Germany was a nation that had great ambition.

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:07.759
Haber was determined to support his country
in World War I, unlike Albert Einstein, who

00:08:07.759 --> 00:08:12.979
was horrified that his fellow scientists used
their skills to help Germany's war effort.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:18.880
Einstein the pacifist put it this way: “Our
entire much-praised technological progress,

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:25.810
and civilization generally, could be compared
to an axe in the hand of a pathological criminal.”

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:32.250
Haber's process for synthesizing ammonia also
enabled the creation of explosives.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:37.669
Synthesized ammonia can be converted into
nitric acid, which can be used to create nitrates,

00:08:37.669 --> 00:08:41.180
essential components in powerful explosives.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:45.769
Because of Haber’s process, Germany could
fight on, despite British warships blocking

00:08:45.769 --> 00:08:48.480
nitrate supplies from South America.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.130
But explosives would not be Haber’s greatest
contribution to his country’s war effort.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:59.600
Haber suggested to German military leaders
that they use chlorine gas as a weapon.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.550
German High Command was willing to try anything
to break the stalemate on the Western front.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:11.700
On April 22, 1915, German troops stationed
in Ypres, Belgium, released 5,000 tanks containing

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.690
chlorine gas which drifted across the battlefield
to Allied positions.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:21.380
A British officer described the horror of
seeing French colonial soldiers “...with

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:27.090
gray faces and protruding eyeballs, clutching
their throats and choking as they ran, many

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:31.920
of them dropping in their tracks and lying
on the sodden earth with limbs convulsed and

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.470
features distorted in death.”

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:39.959
Soldiers panicked and abandoned their positions,
creating a four-mile gap in the frontline

00:09:39.959 --> 00:09:42.480
that the Germans failed to exploit.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:46.600
Several German commanders had actually opposed
using poison gas on the battlefield.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:52.240
They thought it was a dishonorable and inhumane
way to fight as traditionally, soldiers faced

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:54.060
each other directly in combat.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.450
Yet, Haber didn’t consider poison gas any
worse than bullets.

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:03.779
He argued, “...innumerable human lives would
be saved if the war could be ended more quickly

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:04.779
in this way.”

00:10:04.779 --> 00:10:07.070
Unfortunately, it did not speed up the war.

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:12.080
As gas was heavily dependent on the way the
wind blew, and the Allies later used the same

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:13.580
weapon themselves.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:18.490
Haber was promoted to the rank of captain
in recognition of his efforts in developing

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.959
chemical warfare.

00:10:19.959 --> 00:10:21.920
It clearly went to his head.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.519
The first president of Israel quoted him as
saying:

00:10:24.519 --> 00:10:26.680
“I was one of the mightiest men in Germany.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.000
I was more than a great army commander, more
than a captain of industry.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:36.360
I was the founder of industries…All doors
were open to me”.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:41.800
On April 1, 1915, Haber returned to Berlin
to celebrate his promotion

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:46.769
That same night, his wife shot herself with
his army pistol in their garden.

00:10:46.769 --> 00:10:50.100
Their 12-year-old son Hermann found his dying
mother.

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:55.300
There’s been much speculation about why
Clara ended her own life.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:00.400
Decades later, cousin Paul Krassa, wrote:
“She was in despair over the horrible consequences

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:06.089
of gas warfare, for which she’d seen the
preparations, along with the tests on animals.”

00:11:06.089 --> 00:11:08.940
Clara had been miserable for a long time.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:13.300
She struggled with the gender roles expected
of her, leaving her own ambitions to fall

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:14.800
by the wayside.

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:18.959
She detailed her suffering in a letter to
her mentor Richard Abegg, who had introduced

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:20.120
her to her husband.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:25.010
“What Fritz has gained during these last
eight years, I have lost, and what's left

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.990
of me, fills me with the deepest dissatisfaction.”

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:33.850
Haber never publicly defended himself against
speculation that it was his work that caused

00:11:33.850 --> 00:11:35.420
his wife’s death.

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:40.370
We got a glimpse of his emotional state when
he wrote to his former mentor, chemist Carl

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:41.810
Engler, six weeks later:

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:45.120
“For a month I doubted that I could keep
going.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:50.180
But now the war, with its dreadful images
and constant demands on all my powers, has

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:51.800
made me calmer…”

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:57.100
A day after Clara took her own life, Haber
returned to the front lines.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:03.750
He then supervised the development of an even
more horrific weapon – mustard gas.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:08.430
Unlike chlorine gas, mustard gas didn’t
blow away with the wind and instead, stuck

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:14.329
to clothes, causing painful blisters and blinding
soldiers who got it in their eyes.

00:12:14.329 --> 00:12:18.130
Haber called it a “fabulous success.”

00:12:18.130 --> 00:12:23.540
By the end of the war, an estimated 90,000
soldiers had been killed by poison gas, and

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.690
more than a million suffered injuries.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:29.300
Despite Haber’s efforts, Germany lost the
war.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:33.750
The effectiveness of gas diminished as the
Allies started using the same weapon.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:38.910
It didn’t help that Germany made a major
strategic blunder when it began to attack

00:12:38.910 --> 00:12:44.810
both military and civilian ships without distinguishing
between the two, which brought the U.S. into

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:48.079
the war and tilted the balance of power.

00:12:48.079 --> 00:12:52.959
After Germany surrendered, Haber worried that
he’d be tried as a war criminal.

00:12:52.959 --> 00:12:57.209
He moved with his son, his second wife Charlotte,
and their young daughter to neutral Switzerland

00:12:57.209 --> 00:13:01.279
for a time and even grew a beard as a disguise.

00:13:01.279 --> 00:13:06.680
However, instead of being prosecuted for war
crimes, Haber was honored with the Nobel Prize

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:09.649
in Chemistry in 1919.

00:13:09.649 --> 00:13:15.820
The Nobel Prize committee declared his synthesis
of ammonia "the greatest benefit to mankind”.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:20.490
But the award was controversial because of
Haber’s development of chemical weapons.

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:25.180
Two French scientists who had been offered
prizes turned them down in protest.

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:29.250
Although he won the Nobel Prize, his legacy
is forever tarnished.

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:35.690
Haber failed to realize that his nation would
use his chemical weapon against innocent civilians.

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:41.389
The irony is that his own people, the Jewish
people, would become the primary victims.

00:13:41.389 --> 00:13:47.240
The Nazis used Zyklon B to exterminate over
a million people in concentration camps in

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:49.329
World War Two.

00:13:49.329 --> 00:13:53.520
Scientists at Haber’s Institute had developed
an earlier version of Zyklon to get rid of

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:57.430
insect infestations in flour mills and granaries.

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:01.850
Then they introduced another version of the
pesticide that was easier to handle and more

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:06.570
powerful without imagining it would ever be
used on people.

00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:12.960
Haber’s own extended family perished in
Hitler’s gas chambers.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:17.279
Anti-semitism grew after the first world war
as some Germans felt the Jewish people were

00:14:17.279 --> 00:14:20.089
responsible for the country’s significant
problems.

00:14:20.089 --> 00:14:25.830
Hitler’s Nazi party capitalized on the widespread
unemployment, poverty, and unrest to fuel

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:28.050
its rise to power.

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:33.209
Despite his contributions to his beloved Germany,
Haber realized he would soon become a target

00:14:33.209 --> 00:14:35.190
of Nazi repression.

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:40.519
His conversion to Christianity was irrelevant;
it was his ancestry that mattered.

00:14:40.519 --> 00:14:44.769
He was forced to fire some Jewish scientists
at his government-funded research institute

00:14:44.769 --> 00:14:49.940
and, a few days later, Haber resigned his
own position in protest.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:54.560
In his resignation letter to the Prussian
minister of culture, Haber emphasized his

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:55.560
lifelong principle: "My tradition requires
that when choosing coworkers for a scientific

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:56.560
post, I consider only the professional and
personal characteristics of the applicant,

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:57.560
without regard for their racial makeup."

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:00.780
“My tradition requires that when choosing
coworkers for a scientific post, I consider

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:05.949
only the professional and personal characteristics
of the applicant, without regard for their

00:15:05.949 --> 00:15:06.949
racial makeup.

00:15:06.949 --> 00:15:09.920
You cannot expect a man in his sixty-fifth
year to change the thinking that has guided

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:11.009
him for the past thirty-nine years of university
life, and you will understand that the pride

00:15:11.009 --> 00:15:12.110
with which he served his German homeland all
his life now dictates this request for retirement.”

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:16.020
Planck appealed directly to Hitler, trying
to convince him that forcing valuable Jews

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.680
to leave would hurt Germany.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:21.450
Hitler apparently flew into a rage.

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:26.820
Nazi policy left no room for exceptions, not
even for the man who had arguably contributed

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.560
more to Germany’s might during World War
I than anyone else.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:35.199
Einstein, who had been traveling when the
Nazis came to power and declared he wouldn’t

00:15:35.199 --> 00:15:38.560
return to Germany, put it this way in a letter
to Haber:

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:41.100
“I can imagine your inner conflicts.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:45.540
It is somewhat like having to abandon a theory
on which you have worked your whole life.

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:49.740
It’s not the same for me as I never believed
in it in the least.”

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:54.370
Einstein put it more delicately in a letter
to Haber’s son after Haber’s death.

00:15:54.370 --> 00:16:00.279
“It was the tragedy of the German Jew: the
tragedy of unrequited love.”

00:16:00.279 --> 00:16:06.050
Haber tried his entire life to be the perfect
German.

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:10.459
Despite his religious conversion, despite
one of the greatest scientific discoveries

00:16:10.459 --> 00:16:16.230
of all time, despite his attempt to save his
country, the only thing that mattered, in

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:19.050
the end, was that he was Jewish.

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:23.300
He summed up his feelings in a letter to the
prominent German chemist Richard Willstätter:

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:30.560
“I am bitter as never before, and the feeling
that this is unbearable increases by the day.”

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:36.040
As if it could not be any worse, Haber found
himself in a dire financial situation.

00:16:36.040 --> 00:16:41.410
Hyperinflation after World War One had diminished
the value of his royalties from his ammonia

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:42.410
patents.

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:46.329
He also had to financially support his second
wife after their divorce.

00:16:46.329 --> 00:16:51.600
The global financial crisis of 1929 ate away
what was left of his finances.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:55.490
He desperately searched for a way out of his
precarious situation.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:58.730
A former foe actually came to his rescue.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:03.170
British chemist William Pope worked on the
development of gas warfare for the British

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:07.689
and offered Haber a temporary position at
the University of Cambridge.

00:17:07.689 --> 00:17:10.510
By then, Haber’s health had worsened.

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:15.130
He eventually left England to seek treatment
at a sanatorium in Switzerland.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:18.530
He got as far as Basel when his heart failed.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:24.189
On the night of January 29, 1934, Fritz Haber
died.

00:17:24.189 --> 00:17:26.390
He was 65 years old.

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:31.460
Haber had requested to his son Hermann that
this to be written on his gravestone:

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:37.650
“In war and peace, as long as it was granted
him, a servant of his homeland.”

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:40.039
Hermann refused his father’s wishes.

00:17:40.039 --> 00:17:44.600
He also refused to attend his father’s memorial
service believed he couldn’t be honored

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:47.600
in the country that had rejected him.

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:52.560
Scientist Max Planck courageously organized
a service in Berlin on the one-year anniversary

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:57.720
of Haber’s death despite intimidation by
the Nazis, who ordered state employees to

00:17:57.720 --> 00:17:59.100
stay away.

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:02.390
Many of the scientists' wives bravely attended.

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:05.330
The great industrialist Carl Bosch was also
there.

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:07.830
He owed his career to Haber.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:13.330
The Haber-Bosch process led to the stunning
growth of Bosch's company, BASF, which later

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:16.880
merged with other chemical companies to form
the conglomerate I.G.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:21.470
Farben which the Americans seized after WWII.

00:18:21.470 --> 00:18:26.410
Although Haber tried to distance himself from
his Jewish heritage nearly his whole life,

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.810
in his final days, he seemed to have a change
of heart.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:34.170
He struck up a friendship with the Zionist
Chaim Weizmann, who fought to establish the

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:35.200
state of Israel.

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:40.309
Haber lamented to Weizmann: “I was one of
the mightiest men in Germany…At the end

00:18:40.309 --> 00:18:43.520
of my life I find myself a bankrupt.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:49.330
When I am gone and forgotten your work will
stand, a shining monument, in the long history

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:52.580
of our people.”

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:57.289
Despite his significant contribution to the
field of chemistry, there is no statue of

00:18:57.289 --> 00:19:00.230
Fritz Haber in Germany or anywhere.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:04.760
The only place that bears his name is the
institute he once led.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:10.260
The Kaiser Wilhelm Institute was renamed The
Fritz Haber Institute of the Max Planck Society

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:12.270
in 1953.

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:16.790
One of the institute’s directors said that
the name is a reminder that science can be

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:23.330
used as a tool for good and also as a tool
for evil.

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:27.669
Despite the controversy surrounding Fritz
Haber, his contributions to science have left

00:19:27.669 --> 00:19:30.049
a lasting impact on the world

